## 1. Order State Machine

Defined in `/src/model/order.h`:

```cpp
enum class OrderStatus {
    PENDING,   // initial state when Order is constructed
    PAID,      // set when payment succeeds (or, due to bug, prematurely)
    FAILED     // set when inventory reservation or payment fails
};

struct Order {
    std::string id;
    std::string user_id;
    std::string item_id;
    int quantity;
    double price_per_item;
    OrderStatus status = OrderStatus::PENDING;  // default
};
```

Intended state transitions:
- `PENDING` -> `FAILED` (inventory reservation fails)
- `PENDING` -> `PAID` (payment succeeds, after inventory reservation)
- `PENDING` -> `FAILED` (payment fails, after releasing inventory)

---

## 2. Payment Authorization Flow

### Gateway layer (`src/gateway/payment_gateway.h`)
`PaymentGateway` is a pure virtual interface with one method:
```cpp
virtual bool execute_charge(const std::string& user_id, double amount, Currency currency) = 0;
```
`MockPaymentGateway` implements it: returns `true` if `amount <= 1000.0`, `false` otherwise (simulates a charge limit).

### Service layer (`src/service/payment_service.h`)
`PaymentService` wraps the gateway with no logic of its own:
```cpp
bool charge(const std::string& user_id, double amount, Currency currency) {
    return gateway_.execute_charge(user_id, amount, currency);
}
```

### Checkout layer (`src/service/checkout_service.h`)
`CheckoutService` wraps `PaymentService` and always charges in USD:
```cpp
bool checkout(const std::string& user_id, double amount) {
    return payment_.charge(user_id, amount, Currency::USD);
}
```

