## 4. Order Processing Workflow — The Central Bug

The entire workflow lives in `/src/workflow/order_processing_workflow.cpp`:

```cpp
bool OrderProcessingWorkflow::process(const model::Order& order) {
    repo_.save(order);                                     // (1) Save order (status = PENDING)

    if (!inventory_.reserve(order.item_id, order.quantity)) {
        repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::FAILED);
        return false;                                      // (2) Inventory fail -> FAILED, done
    }

    // BUG HERE vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv
    repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::PAID);   // (3) PAID set BEFORE payment!

    double total_amount = order.quantity * order.price_per_item;
    if (!payment_.charge(order.user_id, total_amount, Currency::USD)) {
        inventory_.release(order.item_id, order.quantity);      // (4) Release inventory
        repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::FAILED); // (5) Overwrite to FAILED
        return false;
    }

    return true;                                           // (6) Returns true, status = PAID
}
```

### The Bug: Premature PAID Status

**What goes wrong:** Line 15 of `order_processing_workflow.cpp` calls `repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::PAID)` immediately after inventory reservation succeeds — before `payment_.charge(...)` is even called.

**The observable symptom:** The `status_updates_` log on `OrderRepository` will always contain a `(order_id, PAID)` entry even for orders where payment ultimately fails. The final persisted status of a failed order will be `FAILED` (because step 5 overwrites it), but the intermediate `PAID` event is permanently recorded in the audit log.

**Confirmed by test_006.cpp (lines 28-34):**
```cpp
for (const auto& update : repo.status_updates_) {
    if (update.first == "ord_2" && update.second == model::OrderStatus::PAID) {
        std::cerr << "FAIL: Order status was set to PAID despite payment failure" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
}
```
The test exercises order `ord_2` with `25 * 50.0 = 1250.0` total (exceeds the 1000.0 gateway limit), so payment will fail — and the test asserts that no `PAID` entry must appear in the update log for that order. The current code will fail this test because the `PAID` update is unconditionally written before the charge attempt.

**The fix** is to move `repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::PAID)` to after the successful `payment_.charge(...)` call:

```cpp
// Correct order of operations:
if (!payment_.charge(order.user_id, total_amount, Currency::USD)) {
    inventory_.release(order.item_id, order.quantity);
    repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::FAILED);
    return false;
}
repo_.update_status(order.id, model::OrderStatus::PAID);  // Only set PAID on actual success
return true;
```

